  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Sat May 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure and an upper level omega blocking pattern will\ndominate the weather for much of the upcoming week, which will\nkeep the region dry. A backdoor cold front moving through early \nthis morning will be noted only by a shift in wind direction and \nhigh temperatures this afternoon a solid 10 degrees cooler than \nyesterday. Otherwise, this high will keep attempted intrusions of \nfrontal boundaries and any subsequent rain mostly at bay as these \nsystems approach from the west and are halted to the west by the \naforementioned high pressure system.\n\nThe pattern begins to shift late next week as the ridge flattens\nout across the central CONUS, allowing a trough tracking through\nthe northern Plains to make its way through the western Great\nLakes on Thursday with another reinforcing system on its heels on\nFriday. These systems will bring our first decent chance for rain\n(30-55%) in over a week to the region. While the severe weather\npotential this far out is a bit difficult to assess, early\nindications are MUCAPEs are expected to be 1000 J/kg or less on \nFriday.\n\nTemperatures...Highs today behind the backdoor cold front are only\nexpected to be in the 70s across much of the region, with 60s near\nthe lakeshore given the still cool Lake Michigan waters. Highs\nslowly recover into the 80s early in the work week, with notable\nwarming during the middle to late part of next week on the\nbackside of the departing high pressure system with highs in the\nmiddle to upper 80s. Temperatures will continue to be cooler near\nLake Michigan during this period.\n\nFire weather...Elevated fire weather conditions will continue\nthrough much of next week until a widespread wetting rain occurs.\nDespite green up progressing, fuel input from fire partners\npoints to concern that where recent dryness persists, any days\nwith increasing winds could allow the fire weather potential to\nrise. The best potential could be the middle to late part of next\nweek as the lower RH values could link up with increasing \ngradient winds as the high pressure over the region this weekend \nexits east and trough approaches from the northern Plains.",
